#{[17592186502260 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502227 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502260 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502260 #edb/ref 1071 "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $16$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502260 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70ad5ae1-2125-54c0-a2ba-0b2f8c29700f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502260 #edb/ref 1074 "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{16}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502272 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502311 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502272 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502272 #edb/ref 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $17$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502272 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89c0b61e-c32c-5877-8e81-d049a588ed78" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502272 #edb/ref 1074 "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{17}\\mathbf{a}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502278 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502302 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502278 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502278 #edb/ref 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $3 \\sqrt{11}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502278 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94a87d00-fdd1-5e1d-991b-f4205591c3be" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502278 #edb/ref 1074 "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3 \\sqrt{11}}\\mathbf{a}=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{33}\\mathbf{a}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502283 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502303 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502283 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502283 #edb/ref 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $\\frac{18}{7}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502283 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9dea154a-45e0-5762-a035-b663655cb9ae" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502283 #edb/ref 1074 "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{18}{7}}\\mathbf{a}=\\frac{7}{18}\\mathbf{a}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502297 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502296 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502297 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502297 #edb/ref 1071 "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $5 \\sqrt{15}$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502297 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0356a59-adcb-5ea8-89c2-6b7d2d0fcc0a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502297 #edb/ref 1074 "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{5 \\sqrt{15}}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{75}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502319 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502258 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502319 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502319 #edb/ref 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $4 \\sqrt{13}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502319 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f719072e-c57f-507a-8dda-84c1a653bd39" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502319 #edb/ref 1074 "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{4 \\sqrt{13}}\\mathbf{a}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{52}\\mathbf{a}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502322 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186096813.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502322 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "69c0d337-c0c7-5401-85a9-e535b8ad24e1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502330 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502334 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502334 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d6/d62b7f63213e0a36a4cc186c396de6986f8c9e594fa045791d9c37fad7550f60.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15405bfd-218a-5ebb-ac44-be3eba615aca" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502334 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/ae/ae31ae08afde8173954eef7609108f51e2a5113e7ef37a815511d29e653f33bf.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=0.5$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{2})^2+(\\frac{3}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502340 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502378 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502340 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502340 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/35/35ce1ab5c1689dca152c58e955e4a43c0be0e2bc6a796bf20e84f2c99786f8ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502340 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25059bd6-40b8-5009-96b3-70ae023f7607" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502340 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/9b/9b906579f40ce2a1c59129d80540b339c1bb00fcd9a5f5ce179b01efeb702ab5.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=3.5$ and $BH=0.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{7}{2})^2+(\\frac{1}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502342 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502423 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502342 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502342 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/11/1112fef974be645940a11f8f3f099ca6b0125a6a92ca8642712dde7b86c63ac8.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502342 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e59a005-0b33-5ed7-ad5d-fc08843b1882" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502342 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/4c/4c8930d8535e42d793def554d8e65943e637c02debb6d411048096ba1b8cda68.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=2$ and $BH=0.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2)^2+(\\frac{1}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502356 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502394 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502356 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502356 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/36/364b9ef322e1d6c1aaa54e23a2c09bf086f3721370da7b69aa7036222d1e4318.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502356 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "570883a2-63cb-5ec8-886b-b501a7b97685" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502356 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/89/894f70223b9b29220ee424f53c1ae82ff804fc9b7ca3638e43481d7cea63f66a.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=5$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(5)^2+(\\frac{3}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{109}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{109}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{109}}{109}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502358 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502351 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502358 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502358 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/42/42b28384b26d180eb7ac4fb945a1e75659961be7a4eae1aaed42abe2c861b3ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502358 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59ffdb7f-6502-5ff8-b1d6-0cd16904d80b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502358 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/93/93cd69372903948a36ea13c7db78c1f2df59db19cdb882089263896d7313432e.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=1$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(1)^2+(\\frac{3}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{13}}{13}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502385 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502385 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502385 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1dbd27c1b4987c7b6196da934b586f93062d5e9a77144a0c9defb3f8db9adf17.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502385 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "add307e1-eba2-5029-b8ba-35bea556d853" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502385 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/e8/e81324960632400a3af894d472f054a9e5035dc77ccf8ce87b6720d5af2d5b2f.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=0.5$ and $BH=3$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{2})^2+(3)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{37}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{37}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{37}}{37}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502393 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502411 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502393 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502393 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/da/daa0c47f022b83ba8fe6cd059f7a2971ee92082c9b315f813c93df806b743377.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502393 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3b0065f-ddd8-5f3f-97d3-2bf3c18a2472" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502393 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/ac/ac6e9bcd576c30cd721babe0d12a31cd9a3ba0d4905ad3b02e32d1dd8ca012c9.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=1.5$ and $BH=3.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{3}{2})^2+(\\frac{7}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{49}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{29}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502397 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502426 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502397 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502397 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/02/02112c027add54e9bc7412151f3bd04c31d33e51eb1f96aded05b59e2489950e.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502397 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "baa6d129-80bc-5fdf-803e-edf7f11daa38" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502397 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/d4/d4f6e1f602a4bf57830c11f0814f2fb7d6d1c2304421a32284f6bfb5a92690b5.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=4$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4)^2+(\\frac{3}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{73}}{73}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502398 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502365 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502398 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502398 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b2/b25f39d9bbd30e928d8e07defbed4b2c3e3c4ef46ed92186fb83372dac14b1fd.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502398 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbfa9f4f-6b90-5698-8173-56c2da86ba47" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502398 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/d6/d61b907e6fe5abc9c3e1f3197bfbfc24b7fc67b2e70366877d71d284abf16e85.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=4$ and $BH=3$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4)^2+(3)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16+9} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $5$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502399 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502419 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502399 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502399 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/13/1313a6787b3d2da5ddf917ad9cad72791bc465a265dd73cac8fe9c806afc74ec.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502399 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be9bc2f6-f852-5ff9-bd62-113bf0a7214a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502399 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/fe/fe7fb47997949a3bbf0699ee771a1a05f8c8d7c6558140719d7f7a00316fc231.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=4.5$ and $BH=2.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{9}{2})^2+(\\frac{5}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{106}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{106}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{106}}{53}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502401 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502375 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502401 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502401 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/92/92fcd6bef2b85d707fbe936c7aaa4870b64c158ca12f614123c0957dfa2a0efe.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502401 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c065984c-41d2-5c1b-a564-9053a81a79d9" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502401 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/39/39cc72f1b5da145c6c475c0759ab8df4bead1a136edadfae7c1e17291db451b1.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=1.5$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{3}{2})^2+(2)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{5}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{